<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Dalton</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1766">1766</a>–<a href="/years/1844">1844</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Dalton was an English chemist, physicist and meteorologist whose work laid the foundations of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atomic-theory">atomic theory</a>. Born into a Quaker family in Eaglesfield, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumberland">Cumberland</a> he settled in Manchester and joined its Literary and Philosophical Society in 1794. He proposed that each element consists of identical atoms of characteristic weight, which combine in fixed whole-number proportions, and set out the model in A New System of Chemical Philosophy (1808). He kept daily weather records for over fifty years, formulated the law of partial pressures and gave the first scientific description of colour blindness, still called Daltonism in several languages.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-dalton.jpg" alt="John Dalton" width="330" height="424" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Phillips · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Dalton_by_Thomas_Phillips,_1835.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
